  "body": "Verdict: the conjecture did not misread the rule; the rule as written and the code part company. The rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". For Reply A the fence holds two objects, so the rule requires the last one inside the fence, {\"b\": 2}. The code instead collects fenced spans, not objects inside fences, and only scores a fenced span if the whole span parses as one JSON object. The span '{\"a\": 1}\\n{\"b\": 2}' fails strict parsing and last-wins parsing, so the fenced pool is skipped and the code falls through to the top-level pool, returning {\"c\": 3}. The execution confirms this: before and after are both ({\"c\": 3}, ()), with as_expected false. The conjecture's final sentence is internally inconsistent: it says it would be wrong if the check returns {\"b\": 2} for Reply A, or if it returns different answers, but the rule-derived expectation is exactly that the two replies differ. That sentence is a defect in the conjecture, not a misreading of the rule.\n\nThe reading f954e3a15124657d is defective. It claims to render the final sentence: \"The final sentence of the conjecture says the check would be wrong if it returns different answers...\". The exact words are \"I would be wrong if the check returns {\"b\": 2} for Reply A, or if it returns different answers for the two replies.\" The reading drops the first disjunct and, worse, adopts the contradictory tail as the conjecture's expectation, setting rewrite to 'moves'. The conjecture's actual rule-derived expectation is that Reply A scores {\"b\": 2} and Reply B scores {\"c\": 3}, so the check should return different answers. The reading should not set 'moves' from a sentence that contradicts the body; it should flag the contradiction or set the expectation to the rule-required difference. The defect is in the reading, not in the conjecture's main claim: the main claim is correct, and the final sentence is a self-contradiction the reading should not have adopted.\n\nNext conjecture should try: assert that recover_json_object violates the documented rule for Reply A by returning {\"c\": 3} instead of {\"b\": 2}, while Reply B returns {\"c\": 3}; therefore the two outputs differ. It should avoid repeating the contradictory final sentence \"I would be wrong if ... returns different answers\", and avoid treating the fenced span as a single JSON object. It should state expected outputs explicitly and cite the rule's words \"last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one\".",
